\section{Setup and method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Estimand and estimators.}
Units $i=1,\dots,n$ sit on a graph with adjacency $A$, degrees $d_i$ and mean degree $d$. An assignment $z\in\{0,1\}^n$ gives potential outcomes $Y_i(z)$. The estimand is $\gate=\frac1n\sum_i\,[Y_i(\mathbf 1)-Y_i(\mathbf 0)]$.
\emph{Regression adjustment} builds covariates $\varphi_i(z)$ from the assignment (own treatment $z_i$; treated-neighbour fraction $f_i=d_i^{-1}\sum_j A_{ij}z_j$; and optionally the count $c_i/d$, the two-hop fraction, $z_if_i$, $f_i^2$, a spline in $f_i$), fits $Y_i$ on $\varphi_i$ by OLS with an intercept, and returns $\hgate=\Delta x^{\top}\hat\beta$ with $\Delta x=\frac1n\sum_i[\varphi_i(\mathbf 1)-\varphi_i(\mathbf 0)]$ \citep{chin2019regression,han2023model}.
The \emph{reporting specification} is $\varphi=(z_i,f_i)$, for which $\Delta x=(1,1)$ and $\hgate=\hat\beta_z+\hat\beta_f$; we call it ``regression'' unless a richer specification is named. Every such $\hgate$ is a linear functional $w^{\top}y$ of the outcome vector given $z$.
Baselines are DM, H\'ajek estimators with exposure ``treated and at least a fraction $q\in\{0.75,1\}$ of neighbours treated'' (and the control analogue) \citep{horvitz1952generalization,aronow2017estimating,eckles2017design}, and cluster-randomised DM \citep{ugander2013graph}.

\paragraph{Extrapolation to the corners.}
Both global assignments move every unit's exposure to an end point of $[0,1]$, whereas $f_i$ under Bernoulli($p$) has mean $p$ and standard deviation $\sqrt{p(1-p)/d_i}$. Two quantities follow, one for each part of the error.

\emph{Variance.} With independent noise of variance $\sigma_{\rm abs}^2$, $\mathrm{Var}(\hgate\mid z)=\sigma_{\rm abs}^2\,\|w\|^2=\sigma_{\rm abs}^2 L/n$, with leverage $L=n\|w\|^2=\Delta x^{\top}\hat\Sigma^{-1}\Delta x$ and $\hat\Sigma$ the empirical covariance of $\varphi$. For $\varphi=(z,f)$ with $z\perp f$ on a graph of equal degrees, $\hat\Sigma\approx\mathrm{diag}\big(p(1-p),\,p(1-p)/d\big)$ and
\begin{equation}
L\;\approx\;\frac{1+d}{p(1-p)},\qquad \mathrm{SD}(\hgate)\;\propto\; n^{-1/2}\,(1+d)^{1/2}\,[p(1-p)]^{-1/2}.\label{eq:leverage}
\end{equation}
The variance rises with degree because the exposure distribution narrows and the corner is farther in standard deviations, and is smallest at $p=\tfrac12$.

\emph{Bias.} Suppose $Y_i=a_i+(1-s)z_i+s\,g(f_i)$ with $g(0)=0$, $g(1)=1$ (so $\gate=1$ and $s$ is the spillover share), $f_i$ independent of $z_i$, and $a_i$ unrelated to the assignment. The population OLS slope on $f$ is $s\,\mathrm{Cov}(g(f),f)/\mathrm{Var}(f)$ and the regression's bias is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{bias}\;=\;s\Big[\tfrac{\mathrm{Cov}(g(f),f)}{\mathrm{Var}(f)}-1\Big]\;\approx\;-\tfrac{s}{2}\,g''(p)\,(1-2p)\quad\text{for a narrow exposure distribution}.\label{eq:bias}
\end{equation}
It is the chord from $0$ to $1$ against the local slope at $p$: curvature times the raw distance $(1-p)^2-p^2$ of the corners; it does not involve $d$, which only narrows the support around $p$, and vanishes at $p=\tfrac12$ to second order. For a threshold response the slope is the density of exposures at the threshold, so the bias depends on where the threshold falls relative to a distribution whose width shrinks like $d^{-1/2}$; for the count response (b) it reduces to a dispersion of degrees (Section~\ref{sec:varbias}). We evaluate the exact first expression (a binomial mixture over the realised degrees) for every cell and compare it with the measured bias.
The bias is the response at the corners, which no data inside the exposure support constrain; \S\ref{sec:diag} returns to this.

\paragraph{Hypotheses.}
The five predictions tested below (variance exponents, bias form and degree dependence, minimum at $p=\tfrac12$, effect of cluster designs, and a diagnostic computed from the experiment) were fixed before the runs, together with the grid and the acceptance rule of the diagnostic (the \emph{freeze}); cells added afterwards are called post-freeze and labelled. They are listed in Appendix~\ref{app:grid}.
